#acrfpl("CT") provide the current signals on which power-system protection depends, so the software used to dimension them must produce results that are correct, traceable and fit for delivery to the customer. This project evaluates the #acrs("CT") adequacy calculations of #acrs("SECP") Global, an offline substation-engineering tool, for use in the planning phase. Framed as an evaluation study within design-science research and structured as a single-case study, the work operationalises the release question through four research questions covering numerical correctness, report traceability, coverage of the required devices and report fitness for delivery. Each question is answered with a defined metric and judged by a non-compensatory decision rule, and every finding is recorded through a three-level defect classification that links software cause, numerical effect and the #acrs("IEC") 61869 adequacy benchmark. #acrs("SECP") is compared, case by case, against an independent reference model derived from #acrs("IEC") 61869-2, #acrs("IEC") TR 61869-100 and the applicable device manuals.

Three application–device cases are examined: transformer differential protection with the RET670, line differential protection with the RED670 and overcurrent protection with a REF650 requirement. The line-differential results agree closely with the reference and are releasable subject to disclosure of a small conservative deviation. The transformer-differential suitability decisions are consistent with the reference, yet the required value is numerically incorrect because the tool counts the secondary lead resistance twice; the defect is conservative but departs from the device requirement the report cites. The overcurrent case cannot be computed with the required device, because the REF650 is not selectable and the offered REF630 substitute uses a different dimensioning basis, which leaves a coverage gap. In addition, every generated report cites a superseded edition of #acrs("IEC") 61869-1. Under the non-compensatory decision rule, #acrs("SECP") Global is therefore not releasable without qualification for the planning-phase use case in its present state, although it is already usable for line-differential protection once the identified improvements are addressed.